#
# The Kconfig symbols every wz Zephyr application shares. An application's own
# `Kconfig` sources this file, then Zephyr's (`source "Kconfig.zephyr"`).

menu "wz Zephyr profile"

config WZ_ENTROPY_BOARD_HOOK
	bool "The board supplies its own entropy source"
	help
	  The session draws its cookie nonces and its cookie-signing key through
	  `wzApplicationGetRandom`, which deploy/zephyr-common/wz_board_hooks.c answers
	  from Zephyr's entropy device when the build has one.

	  Select this on a board whose true random number generator is not a Zephyr
	  entropy driver: the board's own source file then defines
	  `int wz_board_entropy_fill(void *buf, size_t len)`, which fills the buffer
	  from the hardware TRNG and returns 0, or a non-zero value when it cannot. It
	  must fail rather than return what the buffer held.

	  Never a way to silence the build on a board that has no source: a board
	  with neither this nor an entropy driver does not compile, by design.

config WZ_CORE_CLOCK_BOARD_HOOK
	bool "The board reads its core clock from the chip's registers"
	help
	  An image is built for one core clock (CONFIG_SYS_CLOCK_HW_CYCLES_PER_SEC) and
	  nothing in the kernel checks that the silicon runs at it. On a chip whose
	  clock tree another core must bring up, a core that was started without that
	  runs at the oscillator and every wait lasts many times what it was asked to.

	  Select this on a board that can read the clock it really runs at: the board's
	  own source file then defines `uint32_t wz_board_core_clock_hz(void)`, which
	  returns that clock in hertz from the registers, or 0 when it cannot tell. The
	  image compares it with the clock it assumes before it waits for anything.

	  A board without this answers 0 to the image and is not checked, which is what
	  every emulated board does.

endmenu
